    "question": {
      "text": "The differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx}-x^2=y$; $y(0)=1$ is to be solved by the modified Euler’s method. With $h=0.1$, the value of $y_1$ correct to four decimal places is ______.",
      "type": "MCQ",
      "options": [
        {
          "label": "A",
          "text": "1.2046"
        },
        {
          "label": "B",
          "text": "1.1058"
        },
        {
          "label": "C",
          "text": "0.9954"
        },
        {
          "label": "D",
          "text": "0.8764"
        }
      ],
      "marks": null,
      "negativeMarks": null,
      "difficulty": null
    },
    "answer": {
      "correctOption": "B",
      "correctText": "1.1058",
      "explanation": "Given\n$$\\frac{dy}{dx}-x^2=y,\\qquad y(0)=1,$$\nso\n$$\\frac{dy}{dx}=x^2+y.$$\nHere $f(x,y)=x^2+y$, $x_0=0$, $y_0=1$, $h=0.1$.\nBy Euler’s method,\n$$y_1^{(0)}=y_0+h f(x_0,y_0)=1.1.$$\nBy the modified Euler method, successive corrections give\n$$y_1^{(1)}=1.1055,$$\n$$y_1^{(2)}=1.1058,$$\nand the next correction remains $1.1058$.\nHence $y_1=1.1058$.\nChoice (B)",
      "status": "confirmed"
    },
